            },
            "authorAssociation": "MEMBER",
            "body": "> 1. How do I disable ANSI colors in the output? Neovim ex-command output doesn't handle them.\r\n\r\nCurrently only by writing custom reporter, i.e. `config.execute.reporter`. It should be possible to use [bundled reporter generator](https://nvim-mini.org/mini.nvim/doc/mini-test.html#minitest.gen_reporter) and tweak its output.\r\n\r\n> 2\\. An `'errorformat'` that can read the error report from `mini.test`.\r\n\r\nThere is none yet that I know of. There is indeed an occasional need to populate the quickfix list, so maybe some day it'll be built-in (most realistically if/when there is 'mini.quickfix').",
            "bodyHTML": "<blockquote>\n<ol dir=\"auto\">\n<li>How do I disable ANSI colors in the output? Neovim ex-command output doesn't handle them.</li>\n</ol>\n</blockquote>\n<p dir=\"auto\">Currently only by writing custom reporter, i.e. <code class=\"notranslate\">config.execute.reporter</code>. It should be possible to use <a href=\"https://nvim-mini.org/mini.nvim/doc/mini-test.html#minitest.gen_reporter\" rel=\"nofollow\">bundled reporter generator</a> and tweak its output.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p dir=\"auto\">2. An <code class=\"notranslate\">'errorformat'</code> that can read the error report from <code class=\"notranslate\">mini.test</code>.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p dir=\"auto\">There is none yet that I know of. There is indeed an occasional need to populate the quickfix list, so maybe some day it'll be built-in (most realistically if/when there is 'mini.quickfix').</p>",
            "bodyText": "How do I disable ANSI colors in the output? Neovim ex-command output doesn't handle them.\n\n\nCurrently only by writing custom reporter, i.e. config.execute.reporter. It should be possible to use bundled reporter generator and tweak its output.\n\n2. An 'errorformat' that can read the error report from mini.test.\n\nThere is none yet that I know of. There is indeed an occasional need to populate the quickfix list, so maybe some day it'll be built-in (most realistically if/when there is 'mini.quickfix').",
